{
  "image_name": "5e5a59b440ad3b23f87c96c5d376da92.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a hierarchy of topological phases across spatial dimension and order, and then shows two routes from an ordinary 3D topological insulator to more exotic higher-order states.\n\nOn the left, phases are arranged on a grid labeled by dimension (1, 2, 3) and order. Familiar first-order examples appear along the top: the 1D Su–Schrieffer–Heeger chain, a 2D Chern insulator with chiral edge modes, and a 3D \\(\\mathbb{Z}_2\\) topological insulator with Dirac-like surface states. Below these, higher-order phases are illustrated by where protected states move to lower-dimensional boundaries: a 2D quadrupole insulator has localized corner modes, while in 3D the protected excitations appear on hinges or corners rather than full surfaces.\n\nThe right side focuses on a 3D topological insulator that respects inversion and time-reversal symmetry. Two transformations are shown. Breaking time-reversal symmetry produces an axion insulator, where the surfaces are gapped and a single chiral mode runs along hinges. Doubling the system instead gives a higher-order topological insulator (HOTI), with counterpropagating hinge channels indicated in two colors. Small band sketches emphasize the shift from gapless Dirac surface cones to gapped or paired surface spectra.",
